{
  "meta": {
    "setup": [
      "Add a Jitterflow endpoint pointing at your CRM's create-contact URL, paced to its rate limit.",
      "Select your Jitterflow API credential on both Jitterflow nodes and paste the endpoint key.",
      "Create a sheet named Leads with columns Email, First name, Last name, Company, Status, Jitterflow job and Note.",
      "Select your Google Sheets credential and sheet on the four Google Sheets nodes, then activate."
    ],
    "description": "Reads new rows from a Google Sheet every hour, cleans and validates each email, and queues the lead in Jitterflow, which delivers to the CRM at its rate limit with retries and idempotency. Each row gets its status and job ID; a daily lane writes CRM rejections from the dead-letter queue back to the sheet."
  },
  "name": "Push new Google Sheets leads to a rate-limited CRM with email checks and error write-back",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "c3c3c3c3-0101-4000-8000-000000000101",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -400,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 600,
        "height": 1100,
        "content": "## Push new Google Sheets leads to a rate-limited CRM with email checks and error write-back\n\n### Who's it for\nTeams that collect leads in a Google Sheet (form exports, event lists, imports) and push them into a CRM whose API has a rate limit. Calling the CRM directly for 500 pasted rows turns into 429 errors and leads that never arrive.\n\n### How it works\n1. Every hour, **Read lead sheet** loads the sheet and **Not sent yet** keeps rows with an empty Status.\n2. **Clean up lead** trims and lowercases the email. **Email looks valid?** marks bad rows \"Invalid email\" instead of sending them.\n3. **Queue lead for the CRM** hands each lead to [Jitterflow](https://jitterflow.io), which delivers them one at a time at the CRM's limit, with retries. The email is the idempotency key, so a lead is never created twice.\n4. **Mark as queued** writes the status and Jitterflow job ID back to the row.\n5. Every morning, the bottom lane reads Jitterflow's dead-letter queue (DLQ) and marks the rows the CRM rejected, with its error.\n\n### Requirements\n- A Jitterflow account and API key. The free Developer plan works.\n- The `n8n-nodes-jitterflow` community node. It is verified by n8n, so it's available on n8n Cloud; on self-hosted n8n, install it from **Settings \u2192 Community Nodes**.\n- A Google Sheet named **Leads** with the columns Email, First name, Last name, Company, Status, Jitterflow job and Note.\n\n### How to set up\n1. In Jitterflow, add an endpoint whose destination URL is your CRM's create-contact API. Set **Deliver one every** to the CRM's limit and put its API key in **Auth header**.\n2. Create a **Jitterflow API** credential, select it on both Jitterflow nodes, and paste the endpoint key into **Endpoint Key**.\n3. Select a Google Sheets credential and your sheet on all four Google Sheets nodes, then activate.\n\n### How to customize\n- Reshape **Payload** on **Queue lead for the CRM** to your CRM's field names.\n- Run it every 15 minutes instead of hourly."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "c3c3c3c3-0102-4000-8000-000000000102",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Step 1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        260,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 600,
        "height": 600,
        "content": "## 1. Read rows that haven't been sent\nThe sheet is the to-do list: a row with an empty **Status** hasn't gone to the CRM yet. Paste in 500 leads and the next run queues all of them."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "c3c3c3c3-0103-4000-8000-000000000103",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Step 2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        880,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 600,
        "height": 600,
        "content": "## 2. Clean and validate\nEmails are trimmed and lowercased, so `Jane@Acme.com ` and `jane@acme.com` count as one lead. A row without a usable email is marked **Invalid email** and skipped."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "c3c3c3c3-0104-4000-8000-000000000104",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Step 3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1500,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 520,
        "height": 600,
        "content": "## 3. Queue, then mark the row\nJitterflow accepts each lead instantly, keyed `lead-<email>`, and paces delivery to the CRM. The row gets **Queued** and the job ID, so the next run skips it."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "c3c3c3c3-0105-4000-8000-000000000105",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Step 4",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        260,
        640
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 780,
        "height": 460,
        "content": "## 4. Each morning, find leads the CRM rejected\nAfter its last retry, Jitterflow moves a lead to the DLQ. Only entries whose payload has `source: lead-sheet` are kept, so other endpoints don't show up here."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "c3c3c3c3-0106-4000-8000-000000000106",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Step 5",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1060,
        640
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 600,
        "height": 460,
        "content": "## 5. Write the error into the sheet\nThe row is found by its job ID and marked **Rejected by CRM**, with the CRM's status code in **Note**. Fix the data, then replay it from the Jitterflow dashboard (replay needs the Pro plan)."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
